===Etymology===
From {{uder|en|fr|néologisme}}, from {{uder|en|grc|νέος||new}} + {{m|grc|λόγος||word}} {{suf|en||ism}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|en|/niːˈɒləd͡ʒɪzəm/|a=RP}}
** {{audio|en|LL-Q1860 (eng)-Vealhurl-neologism.wav|a=Southern England}}
* {{IPA|en|/niˈɑləd͡ʒɪzəm/|a=GA}}

===Noun===
{{en-noun|~}}

# {{lb|en|linguistics|lexicography|countable}} A word or phrase which has recently been [[coin]]ed; a new word or phrase.
#: {{syn|en|coinage|neoterism}}
# {{lb|en|linguistics|lexicography|countable|by extension}} An existing word or phrase which has gained a new meaning.<!-- https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/NEOLOGY%2C-NEONYMY%2C-NEOSEMY%3A-TERMINOLOGICAL-Dru%C5%A3%C4%83/cfd654e4efd4e8351357a44b1bef5c0eb4276937?utm_source=direct_link -->
#: {{syn|en|neosem}}
# {{lb|en|linguistics|uncountable}} The act or instance of coining, or uttering a new word.
#* {{quote-journal|en|author=Erin McKean|title=THE WAY WE LIVE NOW: 8-25-02: ON LANGUAGE; Neologist|magazine=w:The New York Times|url=https://www.nytimes.com/2002/08/25/magazine/the-way-we-live-now-8-25-02-on-language-neologist.html|date=25-08-2002|passage=Neologizing, the practice of coining new words, may seem to be an arcane, specialized activity, but it's everywhere -- and the skillful employment of '''neologism''' is what gives English much of its verve.}}
# {{lb|en|psychiatry}} The newly coined, meaningless words or phrases of someone with a [[psychosis]], usually [[schizophrenia]].
# {{lb|en|theology|historical}} {{syn of|en|neology}}.

====Usage notes====
* There is no precise moment when a word stops being "new", but 15&ndash;20 years is a common cutoff (corresponding to one [[generation]] growing up potentially familiar with the word, depending on how common it is). Acceptance of a word as valid by dictionaries or by a significant portion of the population are sometimes mentioned as additional conditions. Some neologisms become widespread and standard (such as new chemical element names), others remain rare or slangy. (Distinguish from [[protologism]]s, coinages which have not become common.)

